Hi.
I've done a bit of source dive, and I noticed that when rodney tries
to steal something (let's assume the amulet), it goes through this
loop (steal.c, lines 500-502).

        for(otmp = invent; otmp; otmp = otmp->nobj)
            if(otmp->otyp == real || (otmp->otyp == fake && !mtmp-
>iswiz))
                break;

which means that the first amulet of yendor it finds (real or fake),
it's the one that is stolen. My first though was that new picked up
items would go straight to the end of the list which would mean: put a
fake amulet in your main inventory before the real one, get the real
one, and if the wizard should steal one, it will be the fake;

Looking at invent.c, lines 324-332:

        if (flags.invlet_constant || !prev) {
            if (flags.invlet_constant) assigninvlet(obj);
            obj->nobj = invent;         /* insert at beginning */
            invent = obj;
            if (flags.invlet_constant) reorder_invent();
        } else {
            prev->nobj = obj;           /* insert at end */
            obj->nobj = 0;
        }

assuming non-empty main inventory, if the invlet flag is set to false
(which is easy to do since it seems it's just the option "fixinv") it
seems that is the case.

Even assuming the fixinv option is true, all it does is call the
reorder_invent(), which all it seems to do is to sort the inventory by
the object's "invlet" (the object identifying char). So, if I'm
correct, all it takes is to adjust  a fake amulet letter to a letter
preceding the real one?

Not that I intend to exploit this code, but it seems that it would be
a "security risk" in nethack :p

Has anyone ever noticed this?

